{"name":"Coronet Blue","hex":"#59728e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coronet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small crown, such as is worn by a noble.","The ring of tissue between a horse's hoof and its leg."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coronet"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coronet Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Coronet Blue is an American adventure drama series that ran on CBS from May 29 until September 4, 1967."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Coronet_Blue.png/330px-Coronet_Blue.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coronet_Blue"},{"title":"Coronet","blocks":[{"text":"In British heraldry, a coronet is a type of crown that is a mark of rank of non-reigning members of the royal family and peers. In other languages, this distinction is not made, and usually the same word for crown is used irrespective of rank. In this use, the English coronet is a purely technical term for all heraldic images of crowns not used by a sovereign. A coronet is another type of crown, but is reserved for the nobility: dukes, marquesses, earls, viscounts and barons. The specific design and attributes of the crown or coronet signifies the hierarchy and ranking of its owner."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Coronet_EarlOfDevon_PowderhamCastle.jpg/330px-Coronet_EarlOfDevon_PowderhamCastle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coronet"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Helena_Snakenborg%2C_Marchioness_of_Northampton%2C_1603.jpg/500px-Helena_Snakenborg%2C_Marchioness_of_Northampton%2C_1603.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Helena_Snakenborg%2C_Marchioness_of_Northampton%2C_1603.jpg/500px-Helena_Snakenborg%2C_Marchioness_of_Northampton%2C_1603.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portrait of Helena Snakenborg, Marchioness of Northampton, in robes for the 1603 coronation of James I. She wears a marchioness' coronet, which differs from the later standardised design with four leaves and four balls.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHelena_Snakenborg%2C_Marchioness_of_Northampton%2C_1603.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ducal_coronet_of_the_Duke_of_Manchester%2C_Kylemore_Abbey.png/330px-Ducal_coronet_of_the_Duke_of_Manchest